target: internal/report/post.go
verify: go test ./internal/report/ -count=1 -run 'TestTheBranchesNothingReached/a_contaminated'
expect: asserted there is NO PR from an answer it could not read
why:    trusting the probe's stdout AS the PR number makes any byte on it a valid answer, and stdout is shared with every descendant that inherited the fd. Measured: `gh pr list` exited 0 printing `[]` — there is no PR for this branch — while a descendant printed "42", and FindPR answered "PR #42", after which the public disclosure was commented onto a PR that does not exist. The `Drained` flag cannot catch it, because a descendant that writes and then EXITS leaves Wait with no error at all: liveness of the pipe is the wrong oracle and the SHAPE of the answer is the right one. Decoding gh's JSON makes `[]42` and `[{"number":9}]42` unreadable, so contamination reports unknown — and unknown rather than "no PR", because an answer that cannot be read establishes nothing. Note what this entry deliberately does NOT mutate: dropping the `--json`/`-q` flags cannot be caught here at all, because the shims are shell scripts that echo a fixed string and never implement gh's own `-q` reduction. The guard is the decode, so the mutation DISABLES its error branch rather than deleting the decode: removing the decode outright orphans the `encoding/json` import — and ONLY that one; `strconv` stays referenced by `strconv.Itoa(prs[0].Number)`, so the earlier claim that it is orphaned too was a compile-behaviour assertion nobody had run. Measured: deleting just the `json.Unmarshal` block gives exactly one error, `"encoding/json" imported and not used`, and a mutation that does not compile scores broken and tests nothing. With the branch disabled a contaminated answer decodes to nothing and falls to `len(prs) == 0`, which asserts there is NO PR — the cause-never-checked half of the same defect, and what the subtest's second assertion names.
